---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<Layout title="Methodology" description={`How ${site.name} chooses listings, prices them, and handles affiliates.`}>
  <section class="wrap page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">About / methodology</p>
    <h1>How the directory is kept honest.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      {site.name} is a static catalog, not a broker and not a performance service. These rules are
      the product.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap about">
    <div class="about-grid">
      <section class="about-card">
        <h2>Hard price rule</h2>
        <p>{site.priceRule}</p>
        <p>
          Today that means ≤ ${site.priceCapMonthly} per month or ≤ about ${site.priceCapAnnual} per
          year on the retail SKU named in the listing. A first-year $199 that renews at $249 can
          still qualify if $249 is under the annual cap. A $20 intro that renews at $79/month does
          not, even if the landing page looks cheap.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="about-card">
        <h2>Intro versus renewal</h2>
        <p>
          Every listing has a <strong>renewal gotchas</strong> field. Trials that convert, Substack
          grandfathering, holiday “special prices,” and first-year Morningstar-style promos are
          called out in words. If we cannot tell whether a cheaper number is the ongoing rate, we
          say so and tell you to read checkout.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="about-card">
        <h2>No invented performance</h2>
        <p>
          We will describe what a product is for and what it is not. We will not fabricate
          backtests, win rates, subscriber returns, or “this paid for itself in one trade.” Model
          outputs (Snowflakes, stars, fair values, heat maps) are treated as presentations of data
          or opinion, not as evidence that you will beat an index.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="about-card">
        <h2>What we list</h2>
        <p>
          Tools (screeners, charting, fundamentals, fund research) and newsletters, plus a few
          products that are both. Coverage in this build is US-retail-shaped: Finviz Elite, Stock
          Rover Premium, TradingView Essential, TIKR Plus, Koyfin Plus, Simply Wall St Premium,
          Finimize, The Diff, Net Interest, The Overshoot, Behind the Balance Sheet, and Morningstar
          Investor. Higher SKUs from the same vendors (Premium Plus, Direct, Advisor) are mentioned
          only to keep you from buying the wrong invoice.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="about-card">
        <h2>Sources and dates</h2>
        <p>
          Prices are logged with a <code>lastReviewed</code> date and notes pointing at public plan
          pages, vendor posts, or widely cited 2026 roundups. Vendor pages that hide dollars behind
          checkout get a nullable annual field rather than a guessed total (see Koyfin Plus). When
          public sources disagree by a few dollars, we say that.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="about-card">
        <h2>Affiliates and disclosure</h2>
        <p>
          Outbound “go” links use placeholders of the form <code>https://example.com/go/{'{slug}'}</code>.
          They are marked <code>rel="nofollow sponsored"</code>. If a real program is attached later,
          the disclosure in the footer still applies: we may earn a commission; we are not your
          adviser; confirm prices yourself.
        </p>
        <p>{site.disclosure}</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
